Advanced nozzle, shock and compressible-duct study (@fd-advanced-compressible: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Gas-Dynamics-Kulkarni.lingua#r_34YGDV61X77JFYZH8X8NGDBS9W", #source "../sources/Compressible-Flow-NACA-1135.lingua#r_5V6PTWHXNJS515S6PMX7BN2ZHM", #source "../sources/Analytical-Fluid-Mechanics-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_075CGXNA8WHV26ZW53C7XTQ48V", #fluid-dynamics "Advanced-free-surface-wave-and-hydraulic-transition-study.lingua#r_7D92P33JB08JEJ18AW6GX5H46J") { r_71ATSDF27111JJ8AAM358JS35K
**Read:** [Gas Dynamics — Vinayak Kulkarni](../sources/Gas-Dynamics-Kulkarni.lingua#r_34YGDV61X77JFYZH8X8NGDBS9W), complete course and the chosen experimental/characteristic development; [Equations, Tables, and Charts for Compressible Flow — NACA 1135](../sources/Compressible-Flow-NACA-1135.lingua#r_5V6PTWHXNJS515S6PMX7BN2ZHM), complete applicable relations and tables; [Fluid Mechanics — Richard Fitzpatrick](../sources/Analytical-Fluid-Mechanics-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_075CGXNA8WHV26ZW53C7XTQ48V), one- and two-dimensional compressible flow.

Derive the isentropic area relation and shock jump conditions from the conservation laws and thermodynamic model. Reconstruct [An inspectable nozzle with an internal normal shock](../computation/An-inspectable-nozzle-with-an-internal-normal-shock.lingua#r_1759XXT97JP6XN6H6HS5DMHRSW) with an independent method, then map nozzle operating regimes as back pressure changes. Relate shock area to a specified monotone geometry and explain which external-flow features the internal model cannot determine.

Add a carefully bounded extension such as friction, heat transfer, variable heat capacity, an oblique shock system or a shock-tube initial-value problem. Recover the simpler result as a limit and compare with NACA relations where their assumptions apply. Check all conserved fluxes, entropy selection and the relevant characteristic directions.

Use an independent primary experiment or validated benchmark with complete conditions to assess the prediction. Include measurement response and boundary-layer effects in interpreting disagreement. Deliver a regime-aware physical account, not merely a Mach-number field or a solver residual, and identify the additional kinetic/chemical physics needed outside the equilibrium continuum range.

**Continue:** [Advanced free-surface wave and hydraulic-transition study](Advanced-free-surface-wave-and-hydraulic-transition-study.lingua#r_7D92P33JB08JEJ18AW6GX5H46J).

**Return:** [Fluid dynamics — motion, forces, transport and evidence](../Fluid-dynamics.lingua#r_7PQW1WDHK708BFV23MD8ET61VZ).
